      "page_idx": 11,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.064,
            0.377,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.84,
            0.119,
            0.927,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": "Math Talk I"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.119,
            0.825,
            0.187
          ],
          "content": "At which positions will the points X and Y be at their closest? When do you think they will be the farthest? What does your intuition say? Discuss with your classmates."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.193,
            0.884,
            0.237
          ],
          "content": "Now, verify your guesses by placing the points X and Y on the sides and measure how near or far they are."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.239,
            0.884,
            0.282
          ],
          "content": "The distance between X and Y can be obtained by measuring the length of the line XY."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.284,
            0.884,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": "How does the minimum distance between the points X and Y compare to the length of AB?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.33,
            0.885,
            0.419
          ],
          "content": "Change the positions of X and Y to check if there are other positions where they are at their nearest or farthest. You could construct multiple copies of the rectangle and try out various positions of X and Y."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.421,
            0.884,
            0.464
          ],
          "content": "How will you keep track of the lengths XY for different positions of X and Y?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.467,
            0.884,
            0.51
          ],
          "content": "Here is one way of doing it. Suppose here are some of the positions of X and Y that you have considered:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.515,
            0.846,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": "When X is 5 mm away from A and Y is 3 cm away from B, XY = \\_\\_\\_ cm \\_\\_ mm"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.565,
            0.836,
            0.609
          ],
          "content": "When X is 1 cm away from A and Y is 1 cm away from B, XY = cm mm"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.614,
            0.836,
            0.658
          ],
          "content": "When X is 2 cm away from A and Y is 4 cm away from B, XY = \\_\\_\\_ cm \\_\\_\\_ mm and so on."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.659,
            0.885,
            0.726
          ],
          "content": "Is there a shorthand way of writing it down? In all the sentences, only the position of X, Y and the length XY changes. So we could write this as:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.747,
            0.845,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": "| Distance of X from A | Distance of Y from B | LengthofXY |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_11_table_13.jpg"
        },
        {
